<TopicPage slug="subjects/earth-science/expert/planetary-geology">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Planetary geology applies Earth\'s methods to <strong>every body we can reach</strong> — and
    the comparison is the point: Venus shows the greenhouse effect unconstrained, Mars shows
    atmosphere loss, icy moons show ocean worlds. Earth is one experiment in a solar system full
    of controls.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the method, the neighbours, the ocean moons, the crater clock" widget="equationStep" config={planetCfg} />

  <h2>What the comparison taught about Earth</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Venus is the cautionary twin:</strong> nearly Earth\'s size and composition, but a runaway greenhouse evaporated its water (no carbon cycle to buffer). The lesson is not "Venus predicts Earth" — it is that climate states are multiple and transitions between them exist.</li>
    <li><strong>Mars is the water world that ended:</strong> valley networks, clay minerals, deltas — a thick atmosphere lost when the dynamo died (~4 Gya), the solar wind stripped it. Magnetism is the shield (the physics lesson\'s field, planetarily priced).</li>
    <li><strong>The Moon is Earth\'s archive:</strong> no atmosphere, no plate tectonics — its surface preserves 4.5 billion years of history Earth erased; Apollo samples still anchor solar-system chronology (the geologic-time lesson\'s calibration).</li>
    <li><strong>The frontier is in-situ:</strong> Perseverance caches Martian samples for return; Dragonfly flies to Titan (2028); Europa Clipper (launched 2024) will taste a plume. Ocean worlds with chemistry and energy raise the astrobiology lesson\'s stakes — is Earth\'s biology the only run of the experiment?</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> NASA and ESA mission archives (Magellan, Cassini-Huygens, Perseverance, Europa Clipper); Planetary Society mission briefs; the astronomy ladder.</p>
</TopicPage>
